is is different from conventional policies like UCP, which determine partition sizes using miss curves only and do not account for memory contention. To study the effect of these partitioning enhancements, we implemented two derivatives of KPart that perform application clustering exactly like KPart, but determine the per-cluster partition sizes differently. The first scheme partitions using speedup curves like KPart, while the second partitions based on per-cluster miss curves (i.e., like UCP but on a per-cluster basis). Neither scheme takes into account bandwidth contention.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nBar chart labeled “Performance gain (%)” comparing SC + BW (blue), SC (orange), and MC (green) for K2, K3, K4, K5, K6, K7, K8. Approximate values: K2 20.0, 19.0, 18.5; K3 21.0, 16.0, 13.0; K4 17.5, 14.5, 10.0; K5 15.5, 9.0, 7.5; K6 13.0, 6.2, 4.0; K7 9.0, 5.0, 2.0; K8 1.5, -0.5, -1.0. Legend: SC + BW, SC, MC.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 8: Comparison of partitioning techniques: speedup curve partitioning with bandwidth contention estimation (SC + BW), speedup curve partitioning (SC), and miss curve partitioning (MC).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) KPart: A Hybrid Cache Partitioning-Sharing Technique for Commodity Multicores (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/324100978_KPart_A_Hybrid_Cache_Partitioning-Sharing_Technique_for_Commodity_Multicores)\nciteturn290search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; §MIT Computer Science and Artiﬁcial Intelligence Lab ‡Qatar Computing Research Institute, HBKU †Oracle Labs ... memory bandwidth contention to partition cache capacity. ... Fig. 8 compares KPart with the two simpliﬁed schemes. ... KPart with a QoS-oriented cache partitioning policy, such\n\n# KPart: A Hybrid Cache Partitioning-Sharing Technique for Commodity Multicores\n\n  * February 2018\n\nDOI:10.1109/HPCA.2018.00019\n\nNosayba El-Sayed\n\n  * University of Toronto\n\nAnurag Mukkara\n\nAnurag Mukk